<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Capetian dynasty</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/987">987</a>–<a href="/years/1848">1848</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as House of Capet</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Capetian dynasty was the royal house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> descended from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-capet">Hugh Capet</a>, who became king in 987. Its direct line ruled until 1328, and its cadet branches, the Valois and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-bourbon">Bourbons</a>, held the French throne until 1848, while other branches ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, Portugal and elsewhere. Beginning with a small royal domain, the Capetians gradually extended royal power over the whole kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/capetian-dynasty.jpg" alt="Capetian dynasty" width="320" height="490" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sodacan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arms_of_the_Kingdom_of_France_(Ancien).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The early <b>Capetians</b> survived by holding on to what they had.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>His grandson Hugh Capet later founded the <b>Capetian dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-15">15 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>He died a year later without an heir, and the nobles elected Hugh Capet, founder of the <b>Capetian dynasty</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-2">2 March in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The county lay on the frontier between Normandy, Maine and the royal domain, and its counts balanced between the Anglo-Norman kings and the <b>Capetians</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-perche">County of Perche</a></li></ul></section>
